  "response": "Apportioning responsibility for a transformer failure between the solid insulation and the network requires evidence independent of either party's commercial interest. The utility attributed the failure to a manufacturing defect in the winding insulation, whereas the equipment supplier attributed the failure to a switching transient originating on the utility's network. The investigative task is thus to determine whether the damage accumulated internally over years of service or was imposed from outside within microseconds.\n\nThe first line of enquiry is chemical. Dissolved gas analysis of the insulating oil quantifies fault gases such as hydrogen, methane, ethylene and acetylene, and those measured quantities show that a high acetylene concentration in transformer oil marks a high-energy arcing fault. Acetylene in transformer oil is produced by the low-temperature oxidation of cellulose, and that account of its origin cannot be squared with reading an elevated acetylene concentration as the signature of a high-energy arc; one of the two readings must be given up.\n\nWhereas the gas record points to a high-energy arcing fault, the condition of the solid insulation speaks to slow thermal ageing: the average degree of polymerisation of the winding paper had fallen to 250. That is to say, the cellulose chains in the winding paper had degraded to a mean length of 250 monomer units. New kraft insulating paper has a degree of polymerisation of approximately 200, and that figure cannot be reconciled with treating a mean chain length of 250 monomer units as the product of degradation in service.\n\nThe external hypothesis is tested on the network side of the terminals, where the relevant evidence is electrical rather than chemical. Digital fault recorders at the substation provide time-stamped voltage and current waveforms for the failure event, and the availability of those waveforms leads to the use of electromagnetic transient simulation software to reconstruct the overvoltage waveform at the transformer terminals. Energisation of a nearby capacitor bank generates transient overvoltages on the connected network.\n\nA further and largely independent check is available at the station itself. Surge arresters fitted with operation counters record the number of discharge events. For the unit in question, either the counter registered no operations in the month preceding the failure, or it registered at least one operation in that month; one of these two states of the record must obtain, and they cannot both obtain. The transient overvoltages placed on the connected network by capacitor-bank energisation brought the latter about. Taken together, the oil chemistry, the paper condition, the reconstructed terminal overvoltage and the arrester record allow the engineer to assign the failure to the party whose equipment or operating practice the physical evidence actually implicates, rather than to the party with the weaker contractual position.",
